@startuml "Bridge - Retrofit - Class Diagram"
skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0
hide empty members
title A standard arrives over a system that already works

package "What the standard requires — we do not design this" {
  abstract class RegulatoryReport <<Abstraction>> {
    # Engine : IVendorClient
    + Submit(period) : IReadOnlyList<string>
    + SetEngine(IVendorClient) : void
    + EngineName : string
    # {abstract} StatementFor(period) : string
    # virtual DecorateRows(rows) : IReadOnlyList<string>
  }
  class QuarterlyReport <<RefinedAbstraction>>
  class AuditedReport <<RefinedAbstraction>>
  RegulatoryReport <|-- QuarterlyReport
  RegulatoryReport <|-- AuditedReport
}

package "What we already have — nobody rewrites this" {
  interface IVendorClient <<Implementor>> {
    + Name : string
    + Open(database) : string
    + Pull(handle, statement) : IReadOnlyList<string>
    + Release(handle) : void
  }
  class LegacyEngine <<ConcreteImplementor>> {
    + ReportDirectly(statement) : string
  }
  class PurchasedEngine <<ConcreteImplementor>>
  IVendorClient <|.. LegacyEngine
  IVendorClient <|.. PurchasedEngine
}

RegulatoryReport o--> "Engine" IVendorClient

note right of RegulatoryReport
  <b>Submit</b> is the operation the regulation names.
  It exists nowhere in the engine: it is composed
  from Open, Pull and Release.

  <b>Engine</b> is protected, not private, because a
  refinement may need the primitives itself.
end note

note bottom of IVendorClient
  <b>Four members, and no Submit.</b> A test asserts the
  whole list. An implementor that grows to mirror its
  abstraction is the failure GoF warn about on p. 154 —
  the same interface written twice, for an indirection.
end note

note bottom of LegacyEngine
  <b>ReportDirectly</b> is a caller from ten years ago.
  It still works, untouched, and knows of no standard.
  A test runs it, because that is the whole promise.
end note

note as N1
  <b>Bridge, not Adapter.</b> One class fitted to one required
  interface, after the fact, is an Adapter — and should be
  called one. This is a Bridge because both sides are
  families: the standard grows refinements, and a second
  engine sits behind it.

  At 2 x 2 a wrapper per pair costs the same four classes.
  That is the last size at which it does.
end note
@enduml
